# Spawn orchestration vs ECS simulation

## Split of responsibilities

| Layer | Use for | Avoid for |
|-------|---------|-----------|
| **GameObject orchestrators** | Bulk spawn, procedural layout, when to generate/expand | Per-frame combat, collision, movement |
| **ScriptableObject evaluators** | Per-room placement scoring and weights (`RoomTemplateBase` subclasses) | Hot-path simulation |
| **Pure C# libraries** | Grid/placement algorithms testable without Unity scene | Unity lifecycle, spawning |
| **ECS + Burst systems** | Attacks, hurtboxes, enemies, walls, pause, spatial queries on many entities | Picking rooms, spawning dozens of layout pieces |

## Rules

- If something **spawns a lot of elements at once** (rooms, props, enemy waves), control it from a **MonoBehaviour director** — not from an ECS system.
- **Room weights:** subclass **`DefaultRoomEvaluator`**; assign on prefab or catalog **Evaluator Override**. No separate run-level weight-multiplier policy layer.
- **Room generation:** `BuildingRunDirector` + `RoomCatalogAsset` + per-room evaluators — see [LevelGenPlan.md](Assets/DesignDock/LevelGenPlan.md).
- **After spawn / layout commit**, ECS simulates the live map. Systems may **read** layout snapshots; they do **not** own generation or bulk instantiation.
- Prefer **Burst-compatible** `ISystem` / `IJobEntity` for gameplay that scales with entity count.

## Examples

```csharp
// ✅ Director commits layout and spawns rooms (GameObject)
director.StartRun(seed);
director.ExpandRun(additionalRooms);

// ✅ Custom weight via evaluator subclass
public sealed class VaultRoomEvaluator : DefaultRoomEvaluator { ... }

// ✅ ECS simulates after entities exist
[BurstCompile]
public partial struct ConeAttackSystem : ISystem { ... }

// ❌ ECS system that picks room templates and Instantiate()s floor prefabs
// ❌ Separate SO that multiplies all room weights without placement logic
```
